[Music]  okay everybody so this is my  spoiler review of fear street part  1 1994 this was directed by lee  yenyak and written by lee yenyak and  also written by phil grazaday okay so i  have so many conflicting emotions about  this film i was super hyped about it and  what's so interesting about this film is  that it  has such amazing elements of suspense in  it  it has great shocks in it and it also  has really good twists in it and i also  think that  it being a slasher horror it's hard to  pull off the sequences without getting  bored during them or getting repetitive  but i think the strongest thing about  this film is how  well it does its sequences with the  killers in them  now where i had some issues it was more  evident in the beginning of this film  was  some of the songs in it i thought all  the song choices were good but they were  too close together and  really jarring there's a scene where our  main character of dean is on the bus  and it goes from insane in the membrane  to creep by radiohead to a somber song  at the memorial service  literally within 30 seconds of each  other so that was just way too crammed  in and felt  just like it was forcing things in  really quick you also feel that just  with the editing through this film  and it's so hard when you judge editing  right because you don't know if it's  really on the editor or if it's on the  lack of coverage that the  director had gotten and you see a lot  you know these things i just noticed  they kind of are blaring a little more  though in this type of movie where  things aren't syncing up right and  there's a lot of continuity errors  in the editing that could also be you  know a continuity script supervisor  mistake there's a lot of reasons that  can happen but again things just don't  match right so there's just a lot of  bizarre editing in this film but what i  really enjoyed about this story was it's  really good as a first movie i think  it's refreshing that it's a trilogy that  goes in reverse i think that's cool and  unique especially for this genre  and i also think that it's really doing  a good job at building the story about  the witch and the lore and kind of  getting you hyped for the next film and  then the film  even after that when we go to 1966 with  this pastor we hear about  and there was moments that worked so  well scenes that work  so well in this film that remind you of  other films you've seen before  you'll feel that screen vibe in that  scene with dina towards the beginning  where the doorbell keeps ringing and the  killer outside is watching her and  stalking her and then it goes to kate's  house i thought that whole sequence felt  such like a love larger scream and it  worked really well and was  really effectively done and there's a  nuance to it where  all the pacing is really on in these  type of intense sequences to really make  you feel  on the edge of your seat and i also felt  one of the standout moments of suspense  was actually towards the end  when they're connecting all the dots  while they're in this room and the  killer's trying to get in and they're  figuring out that sam needs to die  it felt like the scene from the goonies  with the bone organ that's how intense  it was because you have  these characters trying to quickly  figure out a puzzle in their brain  while something's trying to kill them so  i thought that was a standout scene for  me  now where i'm really conflicted is is  simon  a good comic relief or is he over the  top is he try hard  i still don't know i didn't even know  why i was watching  it i thought there was moments that were  cringe but that could even go to the  writing where the line he says  pervert in a panty shop you know it's  overwritten where they're like  dropping joke after joke on top of each  other where it's feeling forced but then  there's moments i thought were actually  pretty hysterical like when  everyone's having their moments of love  but he's in the bathroom by himself and  they insinuate that he  masturbated to himself i thought that  was hysterical but when i'm talking  about shocks i love  i could not believe that we ended up  seeing kate we kind of  get attached to and start to really like  and she got a lot of screen time too  so i was shocked that she you know was  sliced up that was  a surprise that i was not feeling these  type of surprises in films in a long  time most are usually set up  miles away but those two deaths with her  and then simon getting the axes had  really shocked me and i thought were  really really effective  but where there was some writing issues  from our main lens in  dina dina being her main character is  that it almost felt  silly how the father who's an alcoholic  is mentioned but we never  see him i think like if you compare it  to a similar movie in it  that with the girl in it we see very  important character moments with her  father and it really makes you  sympathize with her where  with dina when she has these big moments  with sam and it will go to the end where  she mentions that father and she's not  really going to be like him  and she had visions that she was when  she wasn't with sam that would have felt  more powerful  if we just had a moment to meet this guy  and kind of really see it from dina's  lens what she's  going through so that was frustrating  and i also thought what was odd was  in the beginning they had dina and josh  feel like siblings but i felt like  middle of this movie they forgot they  were siblings and it didn't feel that  way in particular i think a big missed  moment of that was  that took me out of the film believe it  or not was when kate kissed  josh in front of his sister you know and  dina has no reaction like that is  something that a sibling would always  have a reaction to so  that was weird i just felt like there  was a point where like it wasn't played  like they were siblings enough it was  very odd to me  and i did like that it was campy and it  knew what it was  but that still doesn't let it get away  with some really just not great cliches  and one of those big ones is the cpr  revival that happens here  where sam spits out the water and you're  waiting for her to get back to life it's  done to death in films it's it's like a  no-no  don't do that anymore there's got to be  a different way to have her come back so  that was a little disappointing but  overall  most of this stuff was fresh in this  film which i really appreciated and i  think how they wrote sam's character arc  was really  well done and paid off and how she comes  around to not being afraid to show her  judgmental mother who she really is by  kissing  dina in front of her mom and also that  we got that payoff of that she's not  afraid now to  show her true self to the world so that  i thought worked well  i really like how this film ended and i  think that continues with the shock here  and that it worked and it wasn't just  shocking to shock that you know it was  crazy to me when  c german calls again and that is gillian  jacobs here  and this is leading into the sequel  right and she's like it's not over it's  never over this is why i'm comparing it  to it with pennywise it's very similar  in that sense  and how they talk about this witch you  can't stop her she makes the rules  and that you know big shocking moment  that sam comes downstairs and possessed  by the witch again we get this like  exorcist moment and i love they played  some sound garden here  the day i tried to live it's a perfect  throw to the character of sam  and that she's in that situation of  trying to live  and it leaves that nice cliffhanger of  dina saying i'm going to get you back to  sam while she's possessed and having  that nice post-credits moment with  gillian jacobs character and that we're  gonna see her younger self in the sequel  here so it hyped you up for that which  is next week  also going to this music i love in this  film that they've chosen  not just 